        <!-- PARKED from the rough chapter, for session 3 to mine — do not
             ship any of it unchecked:

        (a) Proof-mass prose (rough subsec-proof-mass).  The kx = ma
            derivation and the capacitive-MEMS description broadly match
            her slides 3–5, but session 3 rewrites from the deck (P-12);
            the piezoelectric/piezoresistive aside is NOT in her deck.

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-proof-mass">
            <title>The Proof Mass: Turning Acceleration into a Signal</title>
            <p>
                Inside every accelerometer is a small <term>proof mass</term> — a tiny
                mass suspended by springs from the sensor's housing.  When the housing
                accelerates, the proof mass tends to stay behind (inertia), so it
                displaces relative to the housing.  The spring pulls it back, and at
                equilibrium the spring force equals the inertial force ... x = ma/k.
                Measuring the displacement of the proof mass gives you the acceleration.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-accel-proof-mass">
                REBUILD per manifest 13x/3 (mass-spring-case diagram with labels);
                the raw pull is images/Day13x-Accelerometer/slide03_4c6909d1.png.
            </figure>
            <p>
                In a capacitive MEMS (Micro-Electro-Mechanical Systems) accelerometer,
                the proof mass and springs are etched from a thin silicon wafer ...
                The proof mass forms one plate of a capacitor, and fixed plates on
                either side form the other plates.  As the mass displaces, the
                capacitance to one fixed plate increases and to the other decreases.
                [C1/C2 differential detail goes to Reference — Gate 1.]
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-accel-mems">
                REBUILD per manifest 13x/5 (MEMS micrograph + mechanism; watch the
                multi-picture composite trap); raw pull: slide05_a4db99e5.png.
            </figure>
        </subsection>

        (b) Block diagram figure.  REBUILD per manifest 13x/7 with the
            "we only use the accelerometer" callout; raw pull:
            images/Day13x-Accelerometer/slide07_70371632.png.  The rough
            chapter (fig-lsm303agr-chip) captioned this image as a module
            photo — it is the block diagram; do not reuse that caption.

        (c) Characteristics-table figure (rough fig-lsm303agr-specs).
            REBUILD per manifest 13x/8 — commit-time image must MASK the
            sensitivity column (plans/day13x.md Part 3).  Raw pulls:
            slide08_e77cd560.png, slide08_15a6b647.png.  The rough caption
            said "±2g full-scale and 12-bit low-power mode" — WRONG twice
            (ground truth §7 items 1–2): low power is 8-bit, and her
            settings (CTRL_REG4 = 0x00) select normal 10-bit mode.

        (d) Breakout schematic figure (rough fig-lsm303agr-breakout).
            REBUILD per manifest 13x/9 (callouts: pull-ups, header,
            regulator, I2C strapping); raw pull: slide09_7c9b0a0a.png.

        (e) Data-format prose (rough subsec-accel-data-format): output is
            16-bit, left-justified two's complement; a = 4·raw/2^16 at
            ±2 g.  The rough version's "In 12-bit low-power mode, only
            bits [15:4] are valid" repeats conviction (2) above — the
            course's mode is normal 10-bit.  Session 3 teaches this per
            plans/day13x.md Part 4 (staged: unsigned byte → sign →
            collapse → committed negative conversion).

        (f) ST transfer-diagram figure (rough fig-accel-i2c-transfers,
            image slide17_c59c860f.png = 13x/17): same base as Day 13's
            transfer-patterns figure; 13x Part 4 beat 5 quotes §6.1.1
            auto-increment against it.
        -->
